00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:34.710
- My name's Ed Schoenfeld,

00:04:34.750 --> 00:04:37.690
I'm the managing partner/creator
of Decoy restaurant.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:40.460
- [Andrew] So where did your
interest in Peking duck begin?

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:43.940
- My interest began with my
overall interest in food,

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:45.990
and cooking in specifically Chinese food.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:48.270
And throughout the late
'60s and early '70s,

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:50.170
I became the cooking student of a

00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:51.670
group of Chinese master chefs.

00:04:51.670 --> 00:04:54.020
I knew that my partners loved Peking duck,

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:56.500
I've always thought that
there was a need for

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:58.650
a Peking duck-focused restaurant that held

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:00.080
itself to a really high standard.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:02.250
Our head chef here, Joe Ng, my partner,

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:04.600
is one of the great Dim
sum chefs of the world.

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:06.050
When you get one of our wrappers,

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:07.610
it's a world-class pancake.

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:10.230
The first step in making a
Peking duck in our kitchen,

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:11.830
we take the inside of the duck,

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:12.960
and we put spices;

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:14.970
scallion, and ginger, and star anis,

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:17.180
and a little cinnamon,
some sugar and some salt,

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:18.510
and then we put a skewer,

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:20.800
so that this flavoring agent stays inside.

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:23.280
We attach the end of a bicycle pump,

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:24.960
and we blow it up like a balloon.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:27.520
It causes the skin, and the fat,

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:29.470
to separate from the meat of the duck.

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:31.450
We take a big pot full of boiling water,

00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:34.690
and we drag it real quickly
through the boiling water

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:36.720
to get the skin to get tight.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:39.240
We marinate the duck, and
we paint it with a syrup.

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:40.880
The syrup is water that has a

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:42.870
little sugar and red vinegar in it.

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:44.837
And we hang the duck up, the skin dries,

00:05:44.837 --> 00:05:47.110
so that the skin is very crispy,

00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:48.390
'cause the goal of Peking duck

00:05:48.390 --> 00:05:50.130
is to have very very crispy skin.

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:52.260
We have a custom-made Peking duck oven.

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:53.870
The ducks cook quite quickly,

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:55.330
they cook in 45 minutes.

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:56.290
When we butcher a duck,

00:05:56.290 --> 00:05:59.190
it starts by taking big
pieces of skin off like this.

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:01.160
And the meat, we lay it out on the plate,

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:03.110
and then we put the pieces of skin on top.

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:06.170
We also garnish our ducks
with classical garnishes,

00:06:06.170 --> 00:06:09.070
which is shredded scallion,
and little batons of cucumber.

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:11.060
Traditionally when you serve Peking Duck,

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:13.390
you use a sauce that
is called Hoisin sauce.

00:06:13.390 --> 00:06:16.490
And then we serve sesame based
sauce, and cranberry sauce.

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:18.880
There are 12 pancakes
that come with one duck.

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:20.250
We take our duck carcasses,

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:22.880
and make a really rich broth from them.

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:25.050
And when our guests sit
down to get a Peking duck,

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:27.210
we bring shot glasses of duck Consomme,

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:28.540
that's how we serve our duck,

00:06:28.540 --> 00:06:30.630
with those sauces and those condiments.

